Error Correcting Codes
- System where you add redundance to data so that it can be recoverable even if it is corrupted
- EE + Information Theory
  - Probabilistic/Randomized Model
- TCS
  - Worst Case Model
- Formal Definition:
  - An injective map that encodes strings such that strings of length k are encoded into strings of length n 
    - The strings are comprised of letters from the same alphabet, call it SIGMA, with Sigma^k denoting a string of length k which is comprised of letters from Sigma
    - The cardinality of Sigma is called q (often times it is 2 as information is transferred in bits)
    - The set of all possible messages that can be sent (domain) is called the MESSAGE SPACE
    - k is the message length or dimension
    - The set of all possible messages that can be outputted (range) is denoted as C
    - If y is in C, then is it a CODEWORD
    - n is the block length
      - Note that n >= k since we are adding redundancies
    - k/n is called RATE
      - Want to add less redundancy for efficiency purposes => k should be close to n (i.e rate is close to 1)
- Errors
  - Should expect errors as it is built to reverse them
  - Assumes up to T errors for some T
  - Assume that it will only be changing characters and not modifying the length
  - If putting string x into the map gives string y, we expect to obtain a string z with the same length as y
    - Want to decode z into x. 
    - Maybe like a matrix transformation that maps R^k to R^n, where k < n, that maps x to y?
      - Find and inverse that maps y to x
      - Since many points are compressed into the same point after undergoing the transformation, perhaps errors and the original 
  - Hamming distance
    - Denoted as Delta(y, z) for two strings y, z of the same length
    - Evaluated as sum([y[i] != z[i] for i in range(len(y))])
  - When using Hamming distance, can represent all possible errors as a circle with radius T (Hamming Ball) around the codeword 
  - Thus, our job is to ensure that each point is within at most one Hamming Ball (Injectivity)
    - The Hamming Distance between two codewords should be AT LEAST 2T (Delta(y, y') > 2T), otherwise the Hamming Balls would overlap
  - Minimum Distance
    - Denoted as d
    - Evaluated as min(Delta(y, y')) for all y, y' in C
  - If T < floor((d-1)/2), then we can decode!
- Choosing codewords
  - Random
    - Very good rate vs distance tradeoff
    - Lacks efficient encoding/decoding algorithms
- Optimal
  - High Rate
  - High Minimum Distance
  - Efficient Algorithms for encoding/decoding
- Linear Error Correcting Codes
  - Encoding algorithm is efficient
  - Think of Strings as vectors and Encoding Map is Linear Transformation (I was right)
  - Basically the entirety of good Error Correcting Codes
  - Sigma = Finite Field of size q
    - Alphabet set will only be a prime power
      - Circumvented as there is a finite set of size 2^L for all positive integers L
  - Encoding takes in a k dimensional vector space in the finite field, outputs a n dimensional vector space in the finite field, and is a linear transformation
    - Letting G be the matrix associated with the transformation, x -> xG (Note x is a row vector, col vector would be Gx)
      - Clearly G is of size k by n
      - Choose G such that is a full rank
    - Then, C is row(G) and is thus a k-dimensional subspace
    - Clearly, it is easy to encode as it's just a matrix-vector multiplication (Time complexity O(k*n) for a matrix of size k by n)
  - Usually, decoding the code with errors is NP-hard
    - However, special choices of G can let the task run in P
  - [n, k, d]_q is used as notation to represent the above
    - Sometimes d is ommitted and it would be written as [n, k]_q
  - Define C^perpendicular as the set of all vectors that are perpendicular to vectors in C
    - It can be constructued as The set of all n-dimension vectors v such that the dot product of v and any vector in C is 0
    - It is a subspace of dimension n-k
    - (C^perp)^perp = C
    - C^perp = [n, n-k]_q
    - Let H be the encoding matrix for C^perp
      - The rows of H are orthogonal to all codewords
        - The matrix-vector product is the zero vector iff the vector is a codeword
        - The dot product of a row in H and the vector iff the vector is a codeword
  - Hamming Weight
    - The Hamming distance between a string and the zero string
      - Thus, the number of non-zero entries in the string
  - d = min Hamming Distance of non-zero codewords
    - By linearity, the Hamming Distance between two distinct codewords is the Hamming Weight of a codeword
    - Conversely, the Hamming Weight of any code word is the Hamming Distance between two pairs of codewords by linearlity
  - d = min non-zero number of columns of H which are linearly dependent
    - Since d = min(Hamming Distance of non-zero codewords), d = min(Hamming Distance of non-zero words z such that zH = zero-vector}
    - If a set of vectors is linearly dependent, there exists a set with at least 1 non-zero constant that satisfies the above. 
    - Since it is the MINIMUM amount of vectors that form a linearly dependent set, all constants are non-zero.
